C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about prophets and leave the reader to choose among them. It says what the author believes the prophets themselves teach about how they spoke, it gives the evidence from their own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words, so that a reader who disagrees knows exactly where."),
("p", "The question is narrow, and the prophets ask it of themselves. By their own statements, how is a word of the LORD known? What can turn an announced oracle aside? And what does the LORD swear, so that nothing turns it? These are not questions imported from outside. Moses raises the first when the people say in their hearts, “How shall we know the word which the LORD hath not spoken?” (Deuteronomy 18:21). Jeremiah raises the second when he stands before the potter’s house (Jeremiah 18:7–10). Isaiah, Malachi and Balaam’s own oracle raise the third (Isaiah 54:9, 10; Malachi 3:6; Numbers 23:19). The volume follows the prophets as they answer."),
("p", "The position is this. The prophets give not one test but several, and each answers a different question. A word is the LORD’s only if it comes from him and not from the prophet’s own heart. It is refused if it leads Israel away from the LORD, whatever sign accompanies it. An announced event must come to pass, unless the LORD has attached a condition to it, as he attached one to Nineveh. And a word the LORD has sworn is a different kind of word from an announcement of judgment or blessing on a nation: it is never turned back. A reader who runs the tests in the wrong order, or applies the test of one kind of word to the other kind, will call true prophets false or false prophets true."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passage, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the position is described in the author’s own words and no view is credited to a writer who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume does not take up the matters its neighbours argue. Revelation 13’s false prophet is read in Prophetic Commentary Volume 2, and the prophet like Moses in Acts 3 and 7 in Acts Commentary Volume 1; this volume cites them. It does not argue the sworn covenants of Abraham, David and the New Covenant, which are read in the Covenantal Commentary Series and in Prophetic Identity Volumes 1 and 3, and it uses their conclusions without re-running them. It makes no claim about the Mystery, which Prophecy & Mystery Volume 2 reads. The prophets are read in Israel’s setting, where they were spoken."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "The prophets give the tests themselves, and the tests are not one rule restated. Where a passage names no test or no condition, the volume says so and builds none."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "A prophet is the LORD’s appointed spokesman to Israel, and a word is his only if it comes from him and not from the prophet’s own heart.", "Chapters 1 and 2"),
    ("2", "A word that leads Israel from the LORD is refused whatever sign comes with it; an announced event that does not come to pass was not the LORD’s word.", "Chapters 3 and 4"),
    ("3", "An announced judgment or blessing on a people carries its own condition; the LORD may turn it aside when the people turn.", "Chapter 5"),
    ("4", "What the LORD swears is another kind of word, and the prophets mark it by its own wording; nothing turns it back.", "Chapter 6"),
  ], [0.12, 0.62, 0.26]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any prophet’s word and ask four questions of it in order: where did it come from, where does it lead, did it come to pass, and did the LORD attach a condition or swear it? If one test could do the work of all four, the thesis fails. If each catches an error the others miss, the thesis stands, and the reader will have seen why the prophets give no single rule."),
]
